# ============================================================================
# PengyOS Core - BusyBox configuration fragment
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this file exists.
#
# Buildroot's default BusyBox config is an embedded-systems config: it enables
# ~140 applets, and the ones no other package claims stay in the image as
# BusyBox's cut-down versions. That is what an agent hits: `find` with no
# `-printf`, `grep` with no `-P`/`--include`, `sed` with no `\b`/`\s`, `awk`
# with no `gensub`, `xargs` with no `-d`, `diff` with no `-r`, `time -v`,
# `unzip -l`, `killall -q`, `losetup -f`, `last -x`, `su -l`, `adduser --shell`.
# They read like the real tools and behave like an initramfs.
#
# Buildroot resolves the overlap by *install order* (BUSYBOX_DEPENDENCIES makes
# coreutils, grep, tar, ... build after BusyBox so their files win). That works
# for the packages Buildroot knows about and for nothing else: a Rust multicall
# coreutils is not in that list, so BusyBox's `ls`/`cp`/`cat` symlinks can
# silently win over uutils' depending on the build graph.
#
# So the overlap is resolved in the config, not by luck: every applet named
# below is compiled out of BusyBox, and the tool is provided by exactly one
# package. Two consequences, both wanted: the applets an agent trips on are
# gone, and BusyBox shrinks by roughly the size of what they cost.
#
# Format: a BusyBox .config fragment, merged by Buildroot
# (BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES). "# CONFIG_X is not set" is the
# kconfig spelling of "off".
# ============================================================================

# --- Core utilities: uutils/coreutils owns these (package/uutils-coreutils) ---
# The multicall `coreutils` binary + one symlink per utility. BusyBox's applets
# for the same names are compiled out so nothing shadows the Rust ones.
# CONFIG_BASE32 is not set
# CONFIG_BASE64 is not set
# CONFIG_BASENAME is not set
# (BusyBox has no BASENC applet; uutils' basenc needs no applet turned off.)
# CONFIG_CAT is not set
# CONFIG_CHGRP is not set
# CONFIG_CHMOD is not set
# CONFIG_CHOWN is not set
# CONFIG_CHROOT is not set
# CONFIG_CKSUM is not set
# CONFIG_CRC32 is not set
# CONFIG_CP is not set
# CONFIG_CUT is not set
# CONFIG_DATE is not set
# CONFIG_DD is not set
# CONFIG_DF is not set
# CONFIG_DIRNAME is not set
# CONFIG_DU is not set
# CONFIG_ECHO is not set
# CONFIG_ENV is not set
# CONFIG_EXPR is not set
# CONFIG_FACTOR is not set
# CONFIG_FALSE is not set
# CONFIG_FOLD is not set
# CONFIG_HEAD is not set
# CONFIG_HOSTID is not set
# CONFIG_ID is not set
# CONFIG_INSTALL is not set
# CONFIG_LINK is not set
# CONFIG_LN is not set
# CONFIG_LOGNAME is not set
# CONFIG_LS is not set
# CONFIG_MD5SUM is not set
# CONFIG_MKDIR is not set
# CONFIG_MKFIFO is not set
# CONFIG_MKNOD is not set
# CONFIG_MKTEMP is not set
# CONFIG_MORE is not set
# CONFIG_MV is not set
# CONFIG_NICE is not set
# CONFIG_NL is not set
# CONFIG_NOHUP is not set
# CONFIG_NPROC is not set
# CONFIG_OD is not set
# CONFIG_PASTE is not set
# CONFIG_PRINTENV is not set
# CONFIG_PRINTF is not set
# CONFIG_PWD is not set
# CONFIG_READLINK is not set
# CONFIG_REALPATH is not set
# CONFIG_RM is not set
# CONFIG_RMDIR is not set
# CONFIG_SEQ is not set
# CONFIG_SHA1SUM is not set
# CONFIG_SHA256SUM is not set
# CONFIG_SHA384SUM is not set
# CONFIG_SHA512SUM is not set
# CONFIG_SHRED is not set
# CONFIG_SLEEP is not set
# CONFIG_SORT is not set
# CONFIG_STTY is not set
# CONFIG_SYNC is not set
# CONFIG_TAIL is not set
# CONFIG_TEE is not set
# CONFIG_TEST is not set
# CONFIG_TOUCH is not set
# CONFIG_TR is not set
# CONFIG_TRUE is not set
# CONFIG_TRUNCATE is not set
